Before requesting or conducting a title search, Issuing Agents should verify that they have the correct legal description or property address and current owner’s name\. This generally includes obtaining:

- Full name\(s\) of the owner\(s\);
- Property address;
- County;
- Copies of any unrecorded maps or surveys;
- Legal description \(from vesting deed\);
- Map/Plat Book references;

Whenever possible, Issuing Agents should try to gather the above information and verify it with the buyer\-borrower, seller, and lender\.

The Company has issued a number of owner’s policies covering land throughout Mississippi\. Using an existing Owner’s Policy issued by the Company as “*base title*” or a “*starter policy*” can save time and money\. However, not all owner’s policies are acceptable for use as base title and Issuing Agents must exercise caution and use good judgment in doing so\.

In order to use a prior policy as base title for search purposes, all of the following requirements must be satisfied:

- Use of the policy is not restricted by Section [1\.14\.02](#_bookmark32) below;
- The amount of the proposed policy and the prior Owner’s Policy fit within the limitations discussed in Sections [1\.14\.04](#_bookmark34) below;
- Standard exceptions 1\-6 must be inserted into the commitment and excepted in the proposed policy, unless all of the applicable requirements to delete the same from the new policy are satisfied;
- All specific exceptions shown in the prior Owner’s Policy must be inserted into the commitment and excepted in the proposed policy; and
- Releases or satisfaction and cancellations of prior deeds of trust have been filed of record\.

If any one or more of the above requirements are not satisfied, then the prior policy cannot be used as base title\.

A title examination based upon a prior Owner’s Policy as base title should begin on or before the effective date of the prior Owner’s Policy\.

The Company maintains a database of previously issued title policies\. To obtain copies of a prior Owner’s Policy issued by the Company, Issuing Agents may use the “Back Title Search” feature available on AgentNet or contact the State Office\. When submitting a request to the State Office for a copy of a prior Owner’s Policy, Issuing Agents must provide at least the following information to assist in the search:

- the name of the insured;
- the legal description or address of the Land;
- the county and state where the land is located; and
- the prior policy serial number, if known\.

To be considered complete, all previously issued owner’s policies must include the policy jacket and a copy of Schedules A and B\.

Under no circumstance may an Issuing Agent rely on any of the following to establish base title:

- a prior lender’s policy, regardless of the amount of the policy;
- a prior policy issued by another title company;
- a prior Owner’s Policy that contains a legal description different from that of the proposed policy;
- a prior Owner’s Policy that is incomplete \(e\.g\., missing the jacket or schedules, etc\.\); or
- a prior title commitment\.

A prior policy that falls into any one of the above categories cannot be used as base title under any circumstance\.

Because government sections are always slightly irregular, fractional section descriptions continue the irregularity\. If precise measurements are required and exact boundary lines are essential, a metes and bounds description should be used\. This will enable the surveyor to:

- determine the exact acreage of the parcel;
- discover conflicts with existing occupation;
- verify whether the property is the subject of differing opinions among surveyors as to location or size; and
- determine whether the metes and bounds description should be substituted for the fractional section description\.

Otherwise, the property may be subject to litigation and possible apportionment of any shortage evinced by the fractional section description\. Apportionment is determined according to surveyor standards\.

A __plat note __is generally any notations on the face of a plat which affects future land use\. Plat notes are usually limited to issues applicable to the subdivision of the land, as set forth in duly adopted local subdivision regulations\. In some instances, the subdivision regulations may require the note to be shown on the face of the plat\. Plat notes should not be based either on non\-platting land use regulations or a generalized concept of general police power\.

Plat notes may include among other things:

- References to existing Homeowners Association covenants, conditions, and restrictions;
- A description of specific rights being conveyed to the future lot owners, such as the ability to access common areas;
- Future land use restrictions, such as heights of structures to avoid creating airport hazards;
- Authority for service providers and emergency vehicles to access private roads within the subdivision; and
- References to local zoning requirements or prior approvals \(for example, a recorded planned unit development\)\.

Sometimes plat notes are used to create restrictions that specifically state that they are to run with the land and be enforceable by area lot owners or otherwise are clearly intended to confer valuable rights\. These restrictions could not be changed by replat, but would require vacation or release of the plat notes by the benefited parties, then replat\.

In Mississippi, it is common for plat notes and surveyor notes to be combined under the heading surveyor notes\.

Subdivision plats usually include notes from the surveyors to provide additional context on the subdivision\.

Surveyor notes may include among other things:

- 
	- 
