Title insurance policies may differ slightly from one another with respect to what specific risks are insured\. It is very important, therefore, for Issuing Agents to check each policy closely to determine the extent of coverage and designate on the face of the commitment the type of policy to be issued\. There are several fundamental risks, which are insured by almost all title insurance policies\. Among these are the following:

- Any defects or encumbrances disclosed by Public Records, but not excepted or excluded by the policy;
- Any defects or encumbrances not disclosed by the Public Records \(unless excepted by the policy\);
- Lack of a right of access to and from the land;
- Errors or mistakes in judgment by the title examiner or Issuing Agent; and
- Litigation costs in defending a challenge to the insured’s title, regardless of the merit of the claim\.
